[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186618814 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "782a9cba-d45c-5695-89d7-786de3267daf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186618380} {:answer/id #uuid "a591e744-a679-5787-9dc4-ce879f7010ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186618451} {:answer/id #uuid "d7ee6dd3-0556-58ad-b766-4d4587173da1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186618529} {:answer/id #uuid "e8d62227-e269-591b-89c9-cfa424e5ceaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186618554} {:answer/id #uuid "e9d0d9da-b686-5b2b-8954-5d75ea22ecc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186618560}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7ee6dd3-0556-58ad-b766-4d4587173da1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186618529} :answer-field/id #uuid "8eef453f-b46b-5ce1-ac80-532b24f681f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186618651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fea89c22-bfbb-56f8-bef9-8e4f55fdd439" :question/math-academy-id "q-118773" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u={x}^{3}$,  then $\\text{d}u=3{x}^{2}\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({x}^{3})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({x}^{3}))|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({a}^{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]
